  {
   "n": 15,
   "type": "MCQ",
   "section": "Technical",
   "tags": "single | Marks +1 / -0.33",
   "html": "<p>For the circuit shown, the switch is closes at <em>t </em>= 0 (after having been open for a very long time).<br>The current <em>i</em>(<em>t</em>) is given by (for <em>t </em>&ge; 0):</p>\n<p><img src=\"media/52/52256b960eb898abbe24.png\"></p>",
   "options": [
    {
     "key": "A",
     "html": "<p><img src=\"media/f3/f3f308922f22e97b1f13.png\"></p>"
    },
    {
     "key": "B",
     "html": "<p><img src=\"media/c4/c420f3a0b05827b348fc.png\"></p>"
    },
    {
     "key": "C",
     "html": "<p><img src=\"media/3f/3fd2bb7c29422140fbd5.png\"></p>"
    },
    {
     "key": "D",
     "html": "<p><img src=\"media/b2/b267ff8cdb91dc506e2d.png\"></p>"
    }
   ],
   "answer": [
    "D"
   ],
   "natRange": null,
   "answerText": "D",
   "marks": {
    "pos": 1,
    "neg": 0.33
   },
   "solution": "<p>Since the switch was open for a long time, the capacitor was initially discharged. After the switch&nbsp;is closed, the capacitor looks initially like a short circuit thus drawing a current of 1 mA. As time&nbsp;progresses the capacitor charges and thus approaches an open circuit so that <em>i</em>(&infin;) = 0. <br>The RC time&nbsp;constant is obtained from the two 20 k&Omega; resistors in parallel and the 10 &mu;F capacitor, or RC = 10<sup>&ndash;1</sup>&nbsp;sec.</p>\n<p><img src=\"media/c7/c76566e3380999e8af79.png\"></p>"
  },
